comments:
Thanks Dinh.
I'm Greg from that discussion: https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/hikari-cp/RgS7J0Xs05U

I'm currently on version 2.2.5 which does not have this problem, but can go back to 2.3 for tests if it helps.
 Ok, just to keep you guys updated.  While this exception does not occur in HikariCP 2.2.5, it is still looking like a MySQL Connector/J issue.  Granted, changes in HikariCP 2.3.0 are tripping over this issue in Connector/J ... and there may be something we can do to avoid this issue (still investigating) ... it _does_ appear to be a Connector/J bug.

Here is what we know so far ...

The "HikariCP utility thread" is an Executor created at [PoolUtilities.java:40](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/blob/2.3.0/hikaricp-common/src/main/java/com/zaxxer/hikari/pool/PoolUtilities.java#L40).

It is only used in [PoolUtilties.getAndSetNetworkTimeout()](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/blob/2.3.0/hikaricp-common/src/main/java/com/zaxxer/hikari/pool/PoolUtilities.java#L188) and [PoolUtilities.setNetworkTimeout()](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/blob/2.3.0/hikaricp-common/src/main/java/com/zaxxer/hikari/pool/PoolUtilities.java#L212).

These methods are in turn, called by:<br>
[BaseHikariPool.addConnection()](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/blob/2.3.0/hikaricp-common/src/main/java/com/zaxxer/hikari/pool/BaseHikariPool.java#L367)<br>
[PoolUtilities.quietlyCloseConnection()](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/blob/2.3.0/hikaricp-common/src/main/java/com/zaxxer/hikari/pool/PoolUtilities.java#L48)<br>
[BaseHikariPool.isConnectionAlive()](https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/blob/2.3.0/hikaricp-common/src/main/java/com/zaxxer/hikari/pool/BaseHikariPool.java#L434)

But in both the Java 6/7 and Java 8 overridden  implementations of `isConnectionAlive()` the calls only occur if a `connectionTestQuery` is being used (instead of JDBC4 `isValid()`).

Looking at `PoolUtilities.setNetworkTimeout()` the call basically looks like this:

``` java
connection.setNetworkTimeout(executorService, (int) timeoutMs);
```

Now, digging into the MySQL `ConnectionImpl.setNetworkTimeout()` method, we have this:

``` java
...
5532    checkClosed();
5533    final MysqlIO mysqlIo = this.io;
5534
5535    executor.execute(new Runnable() {
5536
5537       public void run() {
5538         try {
5539            setSocketTimeout(milliseconds); // for re-connects
5540            mysqlIo.setSocketTimeout(milliseconds);
5541         } catch (SQLException e) {
5542            throw new RuntimeException(e);
5543         }
5544     }
5545   });
...
```

We know from the stacktrace that line `5540` is executed, which calls into `MysqlIO.setSocketTimeout()`.  The entire method is this:

``` java
4888   protected void setSocketTimeout(int milliseconds) throws SQLException {
4889      try {
4890         this.mysqlConnection.setSoTimeout(milliseconds);
4891      } catch (SocketException e) {
4892         SQLException sqlEx = SQLError.createSQLException("Invalid socket timeout value or state", SQLError.SQL_STATE_ILLEGAL_ARGUMENT,
4893         getExceptionInterceptor());
4894         sqlEx.initCause(e);
4895
4896         throw sqlEx;
4897      }
4898  }
```

The stacktrace above indicates an `NullPointerException` at line `4890`.  The only possibility for an NPE occurring on this line is that `this.mysqlConnection` is `null`.

The only place that `mysqlConnection` can become `null` in `MysqlIO` is in the `forceClose()` method:

``` java
/**
  * Forcibly closes the underlying socket to MySQL.
 */
protected final void forceClose() {
   try {
      getNetworkResources().forceClose();
   } finally {
      this.mysqlConnection = null;
      this.mysqlInput = null;
      this.mysqlOutput = null;
   }
}
```

The `forceClose()` method is called under a variety of conditions by MySQL, including failed, aborted, and timed out connections.  It seems the `ConnectionImpl` itself is not marked as closed, otherwise the `checkClosed()` call at line `5532` would have thrown an exception.  What is clealy missing is a guard check at `MysqlIO:4890` like so:

``` java
...
4889      try {
4890         if (this.mysqlConnection != null) {
4891            this.mysqlConnection.setSoTimeout(milliseconds);
4892         }
4893      } catch (SocketException e) {
...
```

Finally, another question is, what can HakariCP do to avoid this issue?  Comparing the 2.2.5 codebase to the 2.3.0 codebase, we find that `addConnection()` and `isConnectionAlive()` are basically the same -- so their uses of the `setNetworkTimeout()` are unlikely to be tripping over the issue.  However, the `PoolUtilities.quietlyCloseConnection()` changed from this:

``` java
try {
   connection.close();
}
catch (SQLException e) {
   return;
}
```

to this:

``` java
try {
   setNetworkTimeout(connection, TimeUnit.SECONDS.toMillis(30), true);
   connection.close();
}
catch (Throwable e) {
   LOGGER.debug("{} - Exception closing connection {}", poolName, connection.toString(), e);
}
```

It seems likely that in some cases, the Connection has been found to be invalid in some way by HikariCP, MySQL has internally called `forceClose()`, and when HikariCP calls `quietlyCloseConnection()` the attempt to set the network timeout results in the NPE inside of the MySQL code.

This change was meant to limit the amount of time that a `connection.close()` might block due to a network interruption.  We can remove this call, but a preferrable solution would be for MySQL to implement the proper guard code in their `setNetworkTimeout()` implementation.
 May be the exception from setNetworkTimeout(connection, TimeUnit.SECONDS.toMillis(30), true) should be caught straight away to allow execution to continue with connection.close()

At least it looks safer for the connection closing purpose of the (quietlyCloseConnection) method.
 IMHO, pool configuration and mysql configuration about various time out values will help. hikaricp debug level log prints it nicely.
one possibility is max age of connection in mysql is less than in pool ?
 @dinhductrong the trouble is that the exception is never thrown from `setNetworkTimeout()`.  Because an `Executor` is ultimately passed into the `Connection.setNetworkTimeout()` call, the exception occurs on the executor thread, not the calling thread.

I've realized that the issue is basically this:
- The `Executor` passed into `setNetworkTimeout()` by HikariCP is a `ThreadPoolExecutor`
- The MySQL implementation calls `executor.execute( new Runnable() ... );` which is "correct", but means that _when_ the `Runnable` is actually executed is non-deterministic.  Meaning the actual setting of the network timeout will occur "at some point in the future"
- HikariCP then immediately calls `connection.close()`.  This results in the Connection being closed "underneath" get `setNetworkTimeout()` call.  If the `setNetworkTimeout()` actually does execute _after_ the `close()`, this issue can arise.

I actually want the calls to `setNetworkTimeout()` to be _synchronous_.  They should complete before execution continues in HikariCP.  The fix is trivial, `Executor` is simply and _interface_ and HikariCP can provide an `Executor` implementation whose `execute()` method simply executes the `Runnable` on the caller thread when `executor.execute(Runnable)` is invoked.

I am 100% this is will solve the issue, and potentially other races that were lurking in the use of `setNetworkTimeout()`.
 Thanks for the details Brett, that is interesting. Good job on hunting this one !
 Thanks Brett, it looks like that's the idea. Sorry I did not try the RCs.
 answer here http://stackoverflow.com/questions/10654547/how-to-use-java-sql-connection-setnetworktimeout looks more convincing to me.
 @nitincchauhan how would that solve the asynchronicity issue ? The answer proposed there is very similar to what Brett detailed above:

> The Executor passed into setNetworkTimeout() by HikariCP is a ThreadPoolExecutor 
 @nitincchauhan @gouegd It wouldn't solve the asynchronicity issue.  The more I look a the MySQL implementation of `setNetworkTimeout()` the more I am convinced it is an improper implementation and misinterpretation of the intent of the Executor.  I intend to open a bug against Connection/J later tonight, but until MySQL makes a fix HikariCP will have to workaround the issue.

