 @yaming116 I guess you are using `0.84.0` and that NPE is tracked in #1646 and fixed in #1648.
Please update Realm to `0.84.1` which includes the fix of the issue.
 @zaki50 ok,thanks
 @RobertoArtiles  I can reproduce your dead lock now. There is a minor problem triggered this in your code, but surely we should fix the root cause in the Realm side as well. Before we release the new version with fix, you can just `cancelTransaction` when exception throws before close the Realm. Or just simply use `Realm.executeTransaction()` instead of calling `beginTransaction` and `commitTransaction`. Even with our fix, the cancel/commit the transaction before close the Realm are highly recommended.

The problem is there are some complex logic mixed with our transaction lock and the java `BaseRealm` lock.

If you are interested, here is the test case to reproduce the issue:

``` java
public void testOpenRealmWhileTransactionInAnotherThread() throws InterruptedException {
    final CountDownLatch realmOpenedInBgLatch = new CountDownLatch(1);
    final CountDownLatch realmClosedInFgLatch = new CountDownLatch(1);
    final CountDownLatch transBeganInBgLatch = new CountDownLatch(1);
    final CountDownLatch finishedLatch = new CountDownLatch(1);

    Thread thread = new Thread(new Runnable() {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            Realm realm = Realm.getInstance(testConfig);
            realmOpenedInBgLatch.countDown();
            try {
                realmClosedInFgLatch.await();
                realm.beginTransaction();
                transBeganInBgLatch.countDown();
                finishedLatch.await();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
                return;
            }
            realm.close();
        }
    });
    thread.start();

    realmOpenedInBgLatch.await();
    testRealm.close();
    realmClosedInFgLatch.countDown();
    transBeganInBgLatch.await();

    testRealm = Realm.getInstance(testConfig);
    finishedLatch.countDown();
}
```
 Great! Thanks. Seems like it fixed a problem for me.
 @beeender But I'm just curious. Shouldn't realm.close() take care of open transactions and just simply cancel them, or is too intrusive?

