comments:
@dapengzhang0, this is related to the "start fails" discussion in the past, although probably much easier to resolve. Where is that other issue that caused us to discuss this?

I agree that it would be quite fair to close the channel (`future.channel().close()`) if the `await()` is interrupted. Since we haven't yet assigned `channel = future.channel();` there doesn't seem to be anything else we'd need to clean up. @ejona86 #6601
>Where is that other issue that caused us to discuss this?

This is because we do not listen distributed objects by default on clients. When a distributed object removed in a remote server, client has no idea. getDistributedObjects makes a remote call and cleans up the leftovers locally. Your solution is the correct workaround for this. 
As a solution, we could add a distributed object listener internally or have a scheduled task to run periodically and collect the leftovers.  

 Yes, distributed object listener internally seems to be a right way.
We implemented DistributedObjectListener and add code below in distributedObjectDestroyed method.

```
        Class c = distributedObjectEvent.getClass();
        Field fld = null;
        try {
            fld = c.getDeclaredField("proxyManager");
            fld.setAccessible(true);
            Object obj = fld.get(distributedObjectEvent);
            if(obj instanceof ProxyManager) {
                ProxyManager pm = (ProxyManager) obj;
                pm.removeProxy(distributedObjectEvent.getServiceName(), (String)distributedObjectEvent.getObjectName());
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
```

