description:
If you run the test below TWICE on mapdb-1.0.6 it throws a nasty Exception on the second run. If it is run a third time, it gets worse.
If the clear() method is removed, then it can be run as many times as one wants.

```
@Test
public void testCorruption()
    throws Exception
{
    final int INSTANCES = 100000;
    File applicationDbFile = new File( databaseDir, "testing" );
    DBMaker maker = DBMaker.newFileDB( applicationDbFile );
    TxMaker txMaker = maker.makeTxMaker();
    DB tx = txMaker.makeTx();
    byte[] data = new byte[128];
    try
    {
        ConcurrentMap<Long, byte[]> map = tx.getHashMap( "persons" );
        map.clear();
        for( int i = 0; i < INSTANCES; i++ )
        {
            map.put( (long) i, data );
        }
        tx.commit();
    }
    catch( RuntimeException ex )
    {
        tx.rollback();
        throw ex;
    }
    finally
    {
        tx.close();
    }
}
```

And the Excepiton is;

```
java.lang.AssertionError: unknown trans log instruction '0' at log offset: 5111834
    at org.mapdb.StoreWAL.replayLogFile(StoreWAL.java:858)
    at org.mapdb.StoreWAL.commit(StoreWAL.java:637)
    at org.mapdb.EngineWrapper.commit(EngineWrapper.java:94)
    at org.mapdb.EngineWrapper.commit(EngineWrapper.java:94)
    at org.mapdb.TxEngine.superCommit(TxEngine.java:310)
    at org.mapdb.TxEngine$Tx.commit(TxEngine.java:558)
    at org.mapdb.EngineWrapper.commit(EngineWrapper.java:94)
    at org.mapdb.TxEngine.commit(TxEngine.java:287)
    at org.mapdb.DB.commit(DB.java:1595)
    at org.mapdb.CorruptionTest.testCorruption(CorruptionTest.java:50)
```

comments:
If the INSTANCES variable is reduced to 50,000, then it will fail on the 4th execution. But increasing it to 250,000 doesn't mean it happens within the same execution.
 Will have a look at that. 

For now I would recommend to create all collections BEFORE doing rollbacks:

```
DBMaker maker = DBMaker.newFileDB( applicationDbFile );

    TxMaker txMaker = maker.makeTxMaker();
    DB tx = txMaker.makeTx();
    tx.getHashMap( "persons" ) //create map
    tx.commit();
    tx = txMaker.makeTx(); // open new tx to add data

    byte[] data = new byte[128];
    try
    {
        ConcurrentMap<Long, byte[]> map = tx.getHashMap( "persons" );

```
 We have noticed the same issue when performing `clear()` on large HashSet-based indexes. Our goal is to completely remove the contents of the index. This seems to be reproducible in the same way with either a `clear()` or `DB.delete()` call, and does not require a rollback.

```
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.AssertionError: unknown trans log instruction '0' at log offset: 917526
    at org.mapdb.StoreWAL.replayLogFile(StoreWAL.java:858)
    at org.mapdb.StoreWAL.commit(StoreWAL.java:637)
    at org.mapdb.EngineWrapper.commit(EngineWrapper.java:94)
    at org.mapdb.EngineWrapper.commit(EngineWrapper.java:94)
    at org.mapdb.DB.commit(DB.java:1595)
    at MapDBWALTester.main(MapDBWALTester.java:31)
```

Test driver to reproduce:

``` java
import java.io.File;
import java.util.Set;
import org.mapdb.DB;
import org.mapdb.DBMaker;

public class MapDBWALTester
{
   public static void main(String[] args)
   {
      File directory = new File(System.getProperty("java.io.tmpdir"));
      directory.mkdirs();
      try
      {
         DB db = DBMaker.newFileDB(new File(directory, "mapdb" + "-" + System.currentTimeMillis()))
            .closeOnJvmShutdown()
            .mmapFileEnable()
            .make();

         Set<String> set = db.getHashSet("SET");

         long i;
         for (i = 0; i < 100_000; i++)
            set.add("Test" + i);

         db.commit();
         set.clear();
         db.commit();
         db.close();
      }
      finally
      {
         directory.delete();
      }
   }
}
```

Empirically, the defect seems to related to the size of the set. This has been tested on version 1.0.6.
 Are there any updates on this issue? Is there a timeline for a fix?
 Not yet. I started 1.0.7 release a few days ago and this is going to be fixed in this release.

