 Ahh OK. Would the level of "garbage" creation have increased in 3.5.7.Final from previous releases?

I originally noticed this as our server was sitting there idling and I saw the GC regularly kicking in - I don't recall seeing that with previous Netty releases; even after the workers had been started/used for actual connections.
 @voiceinsideyou at least in the 3.5.x series it should be the same also before. I think in 3.3.x we was "shutdown" the worker if nothing was left to process but I would need to check if that is true. Anyway let me know if it fix your problem ;)
 OK. We only moved to 3.5.x series 6 weeks ago (from 3.2.7.Final) so I may just not have noticed for one reason or another - and a bit of garbage is a small price to pay to address #240 in any case! Will take a look at the snapshot.
 Doesn't seem to have changed things really. Still churning 5000 objects every few seconds with netty-3.5.8.Final-20120913.075429-3.jar.

Looks like it's just the sheer number of calls to SelectorUtil.select() from the AbstractNioWorker's run method... the garbage objects themselves are all produced inside WindowsSelectorImpl.doSelect() - they are iterators created by SelectorImpl.processDeregisterQueue() and WindowsSelectorImpl.updateSelectedKeys().
 But yes, I see there are definitely fewer created now due to your change to the AbstractNioWorker. I guess it's just the sheer number of calls to SelectorUtil.select.

With default 10ms timeout; 100 calls per thread per second? 16 threads, so 1600 calls per second. Each call creates 2 iterators, 3200 iterator garbage objects created per second, so 192,000 garbage iterators to collect every minute?
 Well I was testing on linux and osx and it really made a difference with this change. So maybe something related to windows only ? I don't have a windows installation here to test :(
But now you say its in doSelect(..) before it was in processSelectedKeys(..) so we at least "fixed" the problem in netty itself. Now it's more in the jdk impl itself.. right ?

What also changed from 3.2.7 to 3.5.7.Final is the timeout we use when do Selector.select(..). In 3.5.7.Final its a way more aggressive then before because we noticed the long timeout was prevent the events to get processed in a fast manner if push many events in netty. What you could try is to adjust this and see how this change it.

-Dorg.jboss.netty.selectTimeout=10

The value is in milliseconds where 10 is the default. We used 500 ms in 3.2.7.Final:

https://github.com/netty/netty/blob/netty-3.2.7.Final/src/main/java/org/jboss/netty/channel/socket/nio/SelectorUtil.java#L38

