I have noticed that using epoll for UDP in general incurs surprisingly high native memory overhead, specifically `(Limits.UIO_MAX_IOV + 1) * Limits.IOV_MAX * 16` bytes per event loop. Maybe that's what you're seeing here and the reason a workaround was produced in the first place? @njhill yeah I wonder if we should try to minimise this a bit. Or at least provide some sort of system property. WDYT ? @normanmaurer I'm going to rerun deploys today, with the original library versions we noticed problems with in production. It's also worth noting that we continuously run DNS lookups which are essentially not cached because the TTL is so low (AWS ElastiCache failover) using netty-dns w/ epoll event loop. I reproduced the memory footprint increase with Netty 4.1.32.Final, no problems with 4.1.31.Final. All using same version of Redisson ( 3.8.2 ) with epoll enabled.

![screenshot from 2019-01-31 12-52-35](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/323497/52052779-c17ba880-2557-11e9-915b-e2a034a00b98.png)
 @johnou 

Same for 4.1.33.Final? @mrniko yes same for 4.1.33.Final. @njhill based on the test deploys this morning I believe something was changed in 4.1.32.Final wrt netty-dns / epoll. @johnou thanks for all the details.

I have a suspect what's going on. Could you try to revert this commit: https://github.com/netty/netty/commit/d728a72e745d840da397e287ab9c59f810cdadb4 and see if it changes anything ?

I suspect this performance optimisation (to use sendmmsg when using the epoll transport) may now have the effect of lazy init `NativeDatagramPacketArray` which was not done before if you only used the `EpollDatagramChannel` for `DnsResolver` but for nothing else. `NativeDatagramPacketArray` is initialised per `EventLoop` that is used and can be quite heavy (as @njhill pointed out before).   @normanmaurer I'll give it a go now.

One thing I noticed is that io.netty.channel.unix.IovArray#IovArray allocates a direct ByteBuffer up front and never deallocates, wouldn't it make more sense if IovArray used the PooledByteBufAllocator and gave back the memory to the pool _after_ a write? @normanmaurer probably not as it would be a very big allocation and so may be costly.  @normanmaurer ~~d728a72e745d840da397e287ab9c59f810cdadb4 doesn't revert cleanly, newDnsQueryLifecycleObserver is missing.~~ I'll work it out. @johnou if you need any help let me know and I will push a branch with this  I think there may have been intermediate commits or a force push, tests are running now. I checked out the 4.1.33 tag, reverted d728a72 but there were compiler errors. Here's the patch to fix the compiler errors.

